# Line endings.
#
# Git is configured on some machines to convert text files to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless
# for source, and fatal for anything Linux has to execute: a shell script with CRLF endings has a
# shebang of "#!/bin/sh\r", which the kernel cannot find, and the container dies at startup with a
# misleading "no such file or directory".
#
# These files end up inside a Linux container, so they keep LF whatever the host prefers.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Everything else: normalise to LF in the repository, leave the working copy to the platform.
*               text=auto
